<p>English w is a consonant. In Hmong, w is always a vowel: the i of "roses", said with your lips spread. <b>tw</b> <i>mid</i> means tail and <b>pw</b> <i>mid</i> means to lie down. Written after a, it is a glide: <b>sawv</b> <i>rising</i> (to get up).</p>
