    id: "yog-nyob",
    title: "Two ways to say \"is\": yog and nyob",
    blurb: "Say what something is with yog, where it is with nyob, and describe with no word for is at all.",
    deck: "About me",
    body: `
<p>English uses "is" for three jobs: "This is the house", "I am in the house", "I am happy". Hmong has a different tool for each.</p>
<h3>yog: X is a Y</h3>
<p>Use <b>yog</b> <i>falling-breathy</i> to say what or who something is. <b>Nov</b> <i>rising</i> means this (here).</p>
{{ex:Nov yog lub tsev.|rising · falling-breathy · high · rising|This is the house.}}
{{ex:Koj yog kuv tus phooj ywg.|falling · falling-breathy · rising · low · falling · falling-breathy|You are my friend.}}
{{ex:Kuv yog Hmoob.|rising · falling-breathy · high|I'm Hmong.}}
<p>The small words <b>lub</b> <i>high</i> and <b>tus</b> <i>low</i> in front of a noun are classifiers, counting words: <b>lub tsev</b> (house), <b>tus phooj ywg</b> (friend). The classifiers lesson explains them; for now, copy each one with its noun.</p>
<h3>nyob: to be somewhere</h3>
<p><b>nyob</b> <i>high</i> means to be in a place, to live, to stay. Use it for where.</p>
{{ex:Kuv nyob hauv lub tsev.|rising · high · rising · high · rising|I'm in the house.}}
{{ex:Kuv nyob Minneapolis.|rising · high · —|I live in Minneapolis.}}
<p>It is also the verb in <b>Kuv nyob zoo</b>, "I'm fine": literally, I stay well. In <b>Kuv nyob hauv lub tsev</b>, the word <b>hauv</b> <i>rising</i> means in, inside.</p>
<p>So ask yourself one question: am I saying what something is, or where it is? What it is takes <b>yog</b>; where it is takes <b>nyob</b>. A third kind of "is", <b>hu ua</b> (is called), comes in the lesson on introducing yourself.</p>
<h3>Describing words need no "is"</h3>
<p>In Hmong a describing word works like a verb by itself. Nothing goes between the person and the description: "I happy", "she kind".</p>
{{ex:Kuv zoo siab.|rising · mid · high|I'm happy.}}
{{ex:Nws zoo siab heev.|low · mid · high · rising|She's very happy.}}
{{ex:Nws siab zoo.|low · high · mid|He's kind.}}
<p>Notice <b>zoo siab</b> (happy) and <b>siab zoo</b> (kind): the same two words in the other order mean something else. <b>heev</b> (very) comes after the describing word.</p>
<div class="tip">Most common mistake: adding yog before a describing word. <b>Kuv yog zoo siab</b> → <b>Kuv zoo siab</b>.</div>
`,
