    id: "questions",
    title: "puas, los, dab tsi, leej twg, qhov twg",
    blurb: "Ask yes-or-no questions with puas and los tsis yog, and ask what, who, where, when and how many.",
    deck: "Questions and connectors",
    body: `
<p>English questions move words around: "You speak English" becomes "Do you speak English?". Hmong keeps the sentence in its order and adds one small word.</p>
<h3>Yes or no: puas before the verb</h3>
<p>Put <b>puas</b> <i>low</i> right before the verb.</p>
{{ex:Koj puas hais lus Askiv?|falling · low · low · low · low · rising|Do you speak English?}}
{{ex:Koj puas nyob zoo?|falling · low · high · mid|Are you well?}}
<p>Word for word, the first one is "you puas speak language English?": <b>lus</b> is language, and <b>lus Askiv</b> is English. Yes is <b>yog</b> ("it is"); for no, the next lesson puts <b>tsis</b> before the verb.</p>
<h3>"... or not?" and the friendly los</h3>
<p>Another way to ask is to put <b>los tsis yog</b> <i>low · low · falling-breathy</i>, "or not?", at the end.</p>
{{ex:Koj noj mov lawm los tsis yog?|falling · falling · rising · creaky · low · low · falling-breathy|Have you eaten yet?}}
<p>A short <b>los</b> at the end asks a question that expects yes, as when a visitor arrives.</p>
{{ex:Koj tuaj los?|falling · falling · low|You've come!}}
<h3>Question words stay put</h3>
<p>English moves "what", "who" and "where" to the front. Hmong leaves the question word where the answer will go.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Hmong</th><th>English</th></tr><tr><td class="k">dab tsi</td><td>what</td></tr><tr><td class="k">leej twg</td><td>who</td></tr><tr><td class="k">qhov twg</td><td>where</td></tr><tr><td class="k">thaum twg</td><td>when</td></tr><tr><td class="k">pes tsawg</td><td>how many, how much</td></tr><tr><td class="k">vim li cas</td><td>why</td></tr><tr><td class="k">li cas</td><td>how</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Koj xav mus qhov twg?|falling · rising · low · rising · falling-breathy|Where do you want to go?}}
{{ex:Chav dej nyob qhov twg?|rising · falling · high · rising · falling-breathy|Where is the bathroom?}}
{{ex:Koj xav noj dab tsi?|falling · rising · falling · high · mid|What do you want to eat?}}
{{ex:Nws yog leej twg?|low · falling-breathy · falling · falling-breathy|Who is she?}}
{{ex:No hu li cas?|mid · mid · mid · low|What is this called?}}
{{ex:Koj hnub nyoog pes tsawg?|falling · high · falling-breathy · low · falling-breathy|How old are you?}}
<p>Read them as "you want go where?" and "she is who?". The answer slots straight in: <b>Kuv xav mus tsev</b>, "I want to go home".</p>
<p>To answer, keep the question and swap the answer in for the question word: <b>Koj xav noj dab tsi?</b> gets <b>Kuv xav noj mov</b>, "I want to eat rice". Nothing moves, so if you can ask, you can already answer.</p>
<div class="tip">Most common mistake: moving the question word to the front, as in English. <b>Qhov twg koj xav mus?</b> → <b>Koj xav mus qhov twg?</b></div>
`,
